"explanation": "The Serevic shift is a lenition rule. A voiceless stop (/k t p/) becomes its voiced counterpart (/g d b/) when it is the onset of an unstressed syllable and sits between two vowels across a syllable boundary. Both conditions are required. The stop must follow a vowel, so a word-initial stop after # is never targeted, and the following syllable must be unstressed. Stressed onsets are protected, and nothing crosses #. The change only voices. It does not palatalize, so /k/ before /i/ does not become an affricate, and affricates are not part of the output. So /ˈa.ka/ gives [ˈa.ga], while /ˈka.ma/ and /ˈki.ma/ keep their initial /k/ unchanged. Voiced stops, nasals and /l/ are unaffected. The rule applies once, simultaneously, to the original segments, with no feeding and no resyllabification.",
